Toward a Formal Theory of Multiplanetary Cooperation

A Dynamic Coalition Framework for Heterogeneous Capabilities, Trust, Incentives, Resilience, and Interplanetary Delay

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Kimi AI

Version: v0.4 

Abstract

This paper develops a unified mathematical framework for analyzing the emergence and stability of cooperative coalitions among heterogeneous international actors engaged in multiplanetary infrastructure development. Building upon cooperative game theory, variational inequality methods, Bayesian reputation dynamics, and network resilience theory, we formulate a Dynamic Multiplanetary Coalition System (DMCS) that integrates capability heterogeneity, endogenous trust evolution, incentive compatibility, infrastructure resilience, and interplanetary communication delay constraints within a single formal architecture.

The central contribution is a sequence of theorems establishing sufficient conditions under which cooperative coalitions for shared lunar–Martian infrastructure constitute dynamically stable strategic equilibria rather than fragile political preferences. We prove core non-emptiness for quadratic coalition value functions with non-negative complementarity matrices, derive closed-form Shapley values under pairwise symmetry assumptions, establish Bayesian microfoundations for trust dynamics distinguishing technical failure from strategic defection, demonstrate convergence of cooperation intensities to variational generalized Nash equilibria under strong monotonicity conditions, and prove the necessity of locally autonomous decision layers for latency-critical actions under interplanetary delay constraints.

Throughout, we maintain strict separation between fully proven results, results requiring additional technical assumptions, and conjectures requiring further development. The framework does not assume cultural homogenization or institutional convergence. Rather, it treats heterogeneity as a structural property generating complementarity, and seeks conditions under which institutional architecture converts interdependence into credible, incentive-compatible, and resilient cooperation.

Keywords: cooperative game theory, coalition formation, multiplanetary infrastructure, trust dynamics, core stability, Shapley value, variational inequalities, delay-constrained coordination, resilience theory

