1. Introduction

1.1 The Fundamental Challenge

The emergence of a permanent human presence beyond Earth will require sustained participation from heterogeneous actors—states, space agencies, private organizations, research institutions, and eventually semi-autonomous settlements. These actors differ substantially in technological capabilities, financial resources, strategic interests, institutional structures, risk tolerances, and cultural backgrounds (Johnson-Freese, 2017; Moltz, 2019).

The central challenge is not simply how to develop lunar or Martian infrastructure, but how to construct a cooperation architecture in which heterogeneous actors have sufficient incentives to participate, contribute, remain mutually interdependent, and avoid destabilizing fragmentation. This requires formal understanding of the conditions under which cooperation becomes strategically stable.

1.2 Prior Work and Research Gap

Existing research has addressed components of this problem. International space cooperation has been studied extensively (Sadeh, 2002; Ehrenfreund et al., 2012). Lunar and Martian governance proposals have multiplied (Jakhu & Pelton, 2017; Bruhns & Haqq-Misra, 2016). Cooperative game theory provides tools for analyzing coalition formation and benefit allocation (Chalkiadakis et al., 2011; Ray, 2007). Network games and dynamical systems theory offer frameworks for understanding strategic interaction on infrastructure networks (Jackson & Zenou, 2015).

Most significantly for our purposes, recent work has begun to address multiplanetary governance directly. A 2026 study in Acta Astronautica examining “Cooperative Sovereignty on Mars” (Ferguson & Haqq-Misra, 2026) draws lessons from the International Telecommunication Union and Universal Postal Union, proposing a distinction between a “Survival Layer” and an “Operational Layer” of Martian governance. The experimental Port of Mars program at Arizona State University (ASU, 2025) has investigated collective action, commons governance, free-riding, and survival dynamics in simulated Martian communities. The Artemis Accords provide an emerging institutional framework for lunar and Martian cooperation, though recent analyses highlight tensions between cooperation and institutional fragmentation arising from their bilateral structure (Jakhu et al., 2024).

However, these approaches generally do not provide a unified dynamic mathematical framework linking heterogeneous inter-actor capabilities, endogenous trust, coalition formation, benefit allocation, infrastructure redundancy, and communication-delay constraints across multiple planetary locations. The research gap we address is:

Can a unified mathematical architecture describe how heterogeneous international actors dynamically form, maintain, modify, and dissolve cooperative coalitions around shared lunar and Martian infrastructure, while explicitly modeling capability complementarity, trust, strategic dependence, benefit allocation, free-riding, risk, and resilience?

This paper takes the first rigorous step toward answering this question by establishing mathematical foundations and proving a first set of existence and stability theorems.

1.3 Central Thesis

We advance the following thesis:

Multiplanetary cooperation need not require institutional or cultural convergence. It can emerge as a dynamically stable equilibrium when heterogeneous actors possess complementary capabilities and the institutional architecture converts interdependence into credible, incentive-compatible, and resilient cooperation.

This thesis distinguishes our approach from both utopian visions of global political unification and pessimistic expectations of inevitable fragmentation. The mathematical objective is to characterize the conditions under which the former is not only normatively desirable but strategically stable.

1.4 Research Questions

The framework addresses five interrelated questions:

Q₁: When does capability heterogeneity create positive coalition surplus?
Q₂: When does this surplus generate a non-empty stable allocation set?
Q₃: How does trust evolve endogenously and affect coalition stability?
Q₄: What mechanisms prevent free-riding while preserving participation by weaker actors?
Q₅: How do communication delays transform the equilibrium structure?

