2. The Dynamic Multiplanetary Coalition System

2.1 Core Definitions

Definition 2.1 (Dynamic Multiplanetary Coalition System).
A Dynamic Multiplanetary Coalition System (DMCS) is a tuple:

\mathcal{M} = (N, K, M, T, G, \tau, \Omega, V, \Pi)

where:

· N = \{1, \ldots, n\} is the finite set of actors;
· K = \{K_i(t)\}_{i \in N} is the time-dependent collection of capability vectors, with K_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^d and K_i^k(t) representing actor i's capability in domain k \in \{1, \ldots, d\} at time t;
· M = \{M_{ij}\}_{i,j \in N} is the collection of complementarity matrices, where M_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d} and M_{ij}^{k\ell} \ge 0 measures the synergy between actor i's capability k and actor j's capability \ell;
· T = [T_{ij}(t)]_{i,j \in N} is the trust matrix, with T_{ij}(t) \in [0,1] representing actor i's trust in actor j at time t;
· G(t) = (N, E(t)) is the time-dependent infrastructure network;
· \tau: E \to \mathbb{R}_+ is the communication delay function;
· \Omega = \prod_{i \in N} \Omega_i is the strategy space, where \Omega_i \subset \mathbb{R}_+ represents actor i's feasible cooperation intensities;
· V: 2^N \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R} is the coalition value function;
· \Pi(t) = \{S_1(t), \ldots, S_m(t)\} is the coalition structure at time t.

Definition 2.2 (Cooperation Intensity).
Each actor i chooses a cooperation intensity c_i(t) \in \Omega_i, where c_i = 0 denotes complete non-participation and c_i = 1 denotes full commitment to the coalition.

2.2 The Coalition Value Function

We adopt a quadratic specification with explicit pairwise symmetry:

Definition 2.3 (Quadratic Coalition Value).
For any coalition S \subseteq N and cooperation profile c = (c_1, \ldots, c_n):

V(S; c) = \sum_{i \in S} c_i \cdot v_i + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S} c_i c_j \cdot s_{ij}

where:

· v_i = a_i^T K_i is the standalone value of actor i's capabilities (with a_i \in \mathbb{R}_+^d);
· s_{ij} = K_i^T M_{ij} K_j is the pairwise complementarity between actors i and j.

Assumption 2.4 (Pairwise Symmetry).
For all i,j \in N:

s_{ij} = s_{ji}

This is equivalent to requiring M_{ij} = M_{ji}^T. The assumption ensures that the pairwise synergy is a property of the pair, not of the ordering.

Remark 2.5 (Higher-Order Interactions).
More sophisticated specifications may include higher-order terms:

V(S; c) = \sum_{i \in S} c_i v_i + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S} c_i c_j s_{ij} + \sum_{\{i,j,k\} \subseteq S} c_i c_j c_k s_{ijk} + \cdots

However, for v0.4, we focus on the quadratic specification and defer higher-order analysis to future work.

2.3 The Heterogeneity–Complementarity Condition

The original statement of an “Axiom” claiming that heterogeneity alone generates positive surplus was too strong; the surplus depends on the actual synergy parameters s_{ij} and the cooperation intensities. We therefore replace the axiom with a condition on parameters that formalizes when heterogeneity is potentially beneficial.

Assumption 2.6 (Heterogeneity–Complementarity Condition).
There exist at least two actors i,j \in N such that:

1. K_i \neq K_j (capability heterogeneity), and
2. s_{ij} > 0 (positive complementarity between their capabilities).

This condition states that the system is not trivially devoid of synergy arising from dissimilar capabilities. It does not, by itself, guarantee that any particular coalition will realize positive surplus; that depends on cooperation intensities and the full pattern of complementarities. The research question becomes: under what institutional mechanisms does this structural condition translate into stable cooperation?

