3. Coalitional Stability: Core Existence and Shapley Values

3.1 Preliminaries

We recall standard definitions from cooperative game theory (Chalkiadakis et al., 2011; Peleg & Sudhölter, 2007).

Definition 3.1 (Superadditivity).
A game (N, V) is superadditive if for all disjoint coalitions S, T \subseteq N:

V(S \cup T) \ge V(S) + V(T)

Definition 3.2 (Convexity).
A game (N, V) is convex if for all S, T \subseteq N:

V(S) + V(T) \le V(S \cup T) + V(S \cap T)

Equivalently, marginal contributions are non-decreasing:

V(S \cup \{i\}) - V(S) \le V(T \cup \{i\}) - V(T)

for all i \in N and S \subseteq T \subseteq N \setminus \{i\}.

Definition 3.3 (Core).
The core of a game (N, V) is:

C(V) = \left\{ x \in \mathbb{R}^n : \sum_{i \in N} x_i = V(N), \quad \sum_{i \in S} x_i \ge V(S) \ \forall S \subseteq N \right\}

Definition 3.4 (Shapley Value).
The Shapley value \varphi(V) = (\varphi_1(V), \ldots, \varphi_n(V)) is:

\varphi_i(V) = \sum_{S \subseteq N \setminus \{i\}} \frac{|S|! (n - |S| - 1)!}{n!} \left[ V(S \cup \{i\}) - V(S) \right]

3.2 Core Existence for the Quadratic DMCS

Theorem 3.5 (Superadditivity).
Consider a DMCS with s_{ij} \ge 0 for all i,j \in N. Then for any fixed cooperation profile c \in \Omega, the game V(\cdot; c) is superadditive.

Proof. For disjoint S, T \subseteq N:

\begin{aligned}
V(S \cup T; c) &= \sum_{i \in S \cup T} c_i v_i + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S \cup T} c_i c_j s_{ij} \\
&= V(S; c) + V(T; c) + \sum_{i \in S, j \in T} c_i c_j s_{ij} \\
&\ge V(S; c) + V(T; c)
\end{aligned}

where the inequality follows from s_{ij} \ge 0 and c_i, c_j \ge 0. \square

Proposition 3.6 (Convexity).
Under the same conditions (s_{ij} \ge 0, c_i \ge 0), the game V(\cdot; c) is convex.

Proof. For i \in N and S \subseteq T \subseteq N \setminus \{i\}:

V(S \cup \{i\}; c) - V(S; c) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in S} c_i c_j s_{ij}

V(T \cup \{i\}; c) - V(T; c) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in T} c_i c_j s_{ij}

The difference is:

\sum_{j \in T \setminus S} c_i c_j s_{ij} \ge 0

establishing convexity. \square

Corollary 3.7 (Core Non-Emptiness).
If s_{ij} \ge 0 for all i,j and c_i \ge 0 for all i, then the core C(V(\cdot; c)) is non-empty.

Proof. Convex games have non-empty cores (Shapley, 1971; Ichiishi, 1981). \square

3.3 Closed-Form Shapley Value

Theorem 3.8 (Shapley Value for Quadratic DMCS).
Under Assumption 2.4 (pairwise symmetry), the Shapley value of the quadratic game V(\cdot; c) is:

\varphi_i(V; c) = c_i v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij}

Proof. The Shapley value is the expected marginal contribution over all orderings. For each ordering, actor i's marginal contribution consists of:

1. Standalone value c_i v_i, added exactly once;
2. Complementarity with each actor j that precedes i in the ordering.

By symmetry of the random ordering, each j \neq i precedes i with probability 1/2. Therefore:

\varphi_i(V; c) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \frac{1}{2} c_i c_j s_{ij}

\square

Corollary 3.9 (Core Membership).
Under the conditions of Proposition 3.6 and Assumption 2.4, the Shapley value lies in the core.

Proof. For convex games, the Shapley value is a core allocation (Shapley, 1971). \square

Corollary 3.10 (Individual Rationality).
For all i \in N:

\varphi_i(V; c) \ge c_i v_i = V(\{i\}; c)

Proof. Immediate from s_{ij} \ge 0. \square

