4. Trust Dynamics

4.1 Bayesian Microfoundations for Trust

We define trust as a posterior belief about another actor's cooperative type, updated through Bayesian inference on observed behavior.

Definition 4.1 (Behavioral Types).
Each actor j has a latent type \theta_j \in \{C, D\}, where C denotes “cooperative” and D denotes “defecting.” Actor i maintains a belief:

T_{ij}(t) = \Pr(\theta_j = C \mid \mathcal{H}_t)

where \mathcal{H}_t is the history of observations up to time t.

Definition 4.2 (Observation Space).
Actor i observes outcomes from the set:

\mathcal{O} = \{C, D, F, A\}

where:

· C: observed cooperation (e.g., timely contribution, resource sharing);
· D: observed strategic defection (e.g., deliberate free-riding, contract violation);
· F: observed technical failure (e.g., system malfunction, capability shortfall);
· A: ambiguous observation (e.g., delayed response, partial compliance).

We now specify the observation likelihoods in a manner that correctly composes the possibilities.

Assumption 4.3 (Two-Stage Observation Process).
Nature first determines whether a technical failure occurs, independently of type:

\Pr(F \mid \theta = C) = \Pr(F \mid \theta = D) = \varepsilon_F \in (0,1).

If no failure occurs (probability 1 - \varepsilon_F), then the outcome is either C or D, with type-dependent probabilities that sum to 1 conditional on no failure:

\Pr(C \mid \theta = C, \text{no } F) = 1 - \varepsilon_C, \quad \Pr(D \mid \theta = C, \text{no } F) = \varepsilon_C,

\Pr(C \mid \theta = D, \text{no } F) = q_C, \quad \Pr(D \mid \theta = D, \text{no } F) = 1 - q_C,

where \varepsilon_C \in (0,1) is the probability that a cooperative type produces a defection signal in the absence of failure (e.g., miscommunication), and q_C \in (0,1) is the probability that a defecting type cooperates strategically.

Thus, the unconditional likelihoods for the informative signals C and D are:

\Pr(C \mid \theta) = (1 - \varepsilon_F) \cdot \Pr(C \mid \theta, \text{no } F),

\Pr(D \mid \theta) = (1 - \varepsilon_F) \cdot \Pr(D \mid \theta, \text{no } F),

and \Pr(F \mid \theta) = \varepsilon_F for both types.

Remark 4.4 (Ambiguous Observations).
For o = A, we may define:

\Pr(A \mid \theta = C) = \varepsilon_A, \quad \Pr(A \mid \theta = D) = \delta_A

with \varepsilon_A, \delta_A \in (0,1). The update follows the same Bayesian formula, with magnitude depending on the likelihood ratio \varepsilon_A / \delta_A. In this paper we focus on C, D, F for clarity; ambiguous observations can be incorporated analogously.

Theorem 4.5 (Bayesian Trust Update).
Under Assumption 4.3, the posterior trust after observing outcome o is:

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} \cdot \Pr(o \mid \theta = C)}{T_{ij} \cdot \Pr(o \mid \theta = C) + (1 - T_{ij}) \cdot \Pr(o \mid \theta = D)}.

Specifically:

T_{ij}^{+} =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{T_{ij}(1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - \varepsilon_C)}{T_{ij}(1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - \varepsilon_C) + (1 - T_{ij})(1 - \varepsilon_F)q_C} & \text{if } o = C, \\[2ex]
\dfrac{T_{ij}(1 - \varepsilon_F)\varepsilon_C}{T_{ij}(1 - \varepsilon_F)\varepsilon_C + (1 - T_{ij})(1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - q_C)} & \text{if } o = D, \\[2ex]
T_{ij} & \text{if } o = F.
\end{cases}

Proof. Direct application of Bayes' rule:

\Pr(\theta = C \mid o) = \frac{\Pr(o \mid \theta = C) \Pr(\theta = C)}{\Pr(o \mid \theta = C) \Pr(\theta = C) + \Pr(o \mid \theta = D) \Pr(\theta = D)}.

Substituting the likelihoods from Assumption 4.3 yields the result. For o = F, the likelihood is \varepsilon_F for both types, so the posterior equals the prior. The factor (1 - \varepsilon_F) cancels in the fractions for o = C and o = D. \square

Remark 4.6 (Trust Asymmetry).
The update exhibits a key property: defection drives trust toward zero (when q_C is small), while cooperation increases trust only gradually. Moreover, technical failure does not affect trust, which is crucial for space applications where failures are common but should not necessarily be interpreted as strategic defection.

4.2 Trust-Adjusted Coalition Value

Definition 4.7 (Trust-Adjusted Value).
The trust-adjusted value of coalition S is:

V(S; c, T) = \sum_{i \in S} c_i v_i + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S} c_i c_j \cdot \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} \cdot s_{ij}.

The factor \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} captures mutual trust: complementarity benefits are realized only when both actors trust each other sufficiently.

Theorem 4.8 (Trust and Core Existence).
If T_{ij}(t) \to T_{ij}^* > 0 for all i,j, then the trust-adjusted game V(\cdot; c, T^*) has a non-empty core.

Proof. Since T_{ij}^* > 0 and s_{ij} \ge 0, the trust-adjusted complementarities \tilde{s}_{ij} = \sqrt{T_{ij}^* T_{ji}^*} s_{ij} are non-negative. By Corollary 3.7, the core is non-empty. \square

