5. Dynamic Stability: Cooperation Intensity Equilibrium

5.1 The Cooperation Intensity Game

Definition 5.1 (Utility Function).
The utility of actor i under cooperation profile c and trust matrix T is:

U_i(c; T) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \frac{\beta_i}{2} c_i^2

where \beta_i > 0 captures convex participation costs.

Remark 5.2 (Relation to Shapley allocation).
Note that the utility function above assumes that each actor receives the full pairwise synergy with each partner, not the Shapley share. This is appropriate for a noncooperative game in which actors directly benefit from others' cooperation. A fully integrated model connecting the cooperative allocation (Section 3) to the noncooperative equilibrium is left for future work.

Definition 5.3 (Constraint Set).
Actor i faces local constraints \Omega_i \subset [0, 1], representing budget, capability, and institutional limitations. Additionally, there exists a coupling constraint:

\Omega_c = \left\{ c \in \mathbb{R}^n : \sum_{i \in N} c_i \le C_{\max} \right\}

representing shared infrastructure capacity.

Definition 5.4 (Generalized Nash Equilibrium).
A profile c^* \in \Omega = \Omega_c \cap \prod_i \Omega_i is a Generalized Nash Equilibrium (GNE) if for all i \in N:

U_i(c_i^*, c_{-i}^*; T) \ge U_i(c_i, c_{-i}^*; T) \quad \forall c_i \in \Omega_i(c_{-i}^*).

5.2 Variational Inequality Formulation

Proposition 5.5 (First-Order Conditions).
Under differentiability, a GNE c^* satisfies the variational inequality:

\langle -F(c^*; T), c - c^* \rangle \ge 0 \quad \forall c \in \Omega

where

F_i(c; T) = \frac{\partial U_i}{\partial c_i} = v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_j \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \beta_i c_i.

Theorem 5.6 (Existence and Uniqueness).
Suppose the operator -F(\cdot; T) is strongly monotone on \Omega: there exists \mu > 0 such that

\langle F(c; T) - F(c'; T), c - c' \rangle \le -\mu \|c - c'\|^2

for all c, c' \in \Omega. Then the variational inequality has a unique solution c^*.

Proof. For strongly monotone operators on closed convex sets, variational inequalities admit unique solutions (Facchinei & Pang, 2003, Theorem 2.3.3). \square

Proposition 5.7 (Sufficient Condition for Strong Monotonicity).
The operator -F is strongly monotone if

\beta_i > \sum_{j \neq i} \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij} \quad \forall i \in N.

Proof. The Jacobian of F has diagonal entries -\beta_i and off-diagonal entries \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij}. The condition is exactly diagonal dominance of the negative Jacobian, which guarantees strong monotonicity of -F. \square

Remark 5.8 (Interpretation and Limitation).
The condition \beta_i > \sum_{j \neq i} \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij} means that participation costs must be sufficiently convex relative to total complementarity benefits. This proves stability exactly in the regime where synergy is weak relative to individual cost. The “interesting” multiplanetary case—strong complementarity, high value—is precisely where this convergence guarantee does not apply. This limitation is acknowledged and discussed further in Section 10.2.

5.3 Convergence Dynamics

We present a discrete-time projected gradient algorithm, which is the natural object when a step-size bound is involved.

Theorem 5.9 (Projected Gradient Convergence, Discrete Time).
Consider the discrete projected gradient iteration:

c^{k+1} = \Pi_{\Omega} \left[ c^k + \alpha F(c^k; T) \right],

where \Pi_{\Omega} is the Euclidean projection onto \Omega and \alpha > 0 is a step size. Suppose -F is strongly monotone with modulus \mu > 0 and F is Lipschitz continuous with constant L > 0. If \alpha \in (0, 2\mu / L^2), then the sequence \{c^k\} converges linearly to the unique GNE c^*.

Proof. This is a standard result in the theory of projected gradient methods for variational inequalities (Facchinei & Pang, 2003, Chapter 12). The strong monotonicity of -F ensures that the mapping c \mapsto \Pi_{\Omega}[c + \alpha F(c)] is a contraction for sufficiently small \alpha, yielding convergence. \square

Theorem 5.10 (Stability of Cooperative Equilibrium).
Let c^* be the unique GNE at which all actors participate with positive intensity (c_i^* > 0 for all i). Then c^* is a locally asymptotically stable fixed point of the discrete projected gradient dynamics of Theorem 5.9.

Proof. Immediate from the contraction property. \square

