6. Anti-Free-Riding Mechanisms

6.1 The Free-Riding Problem

Free-riding occurs when an actor receives benefits from coalition membership while contributing less than their fair share. In the noncooperative game of Section 5, this manifests as setting c_i low while benefiting from high c_j of others.

Proposition 6.1 (Free-Riding Incentive at the Cooperative Optimum).
Let c^{\text{coop}} be the vector of cooperation intensities that maximizes the total surplus \sum_i U_i(c;T) subject to constraints. At c^{\text{coop}}, actor i has an incentive to unilaterally reduce c_i below c_i^{\text{coop}} if

\frac{\partial U_i}{\partial c_i}\bigg|_{c = c^{\text{coop}}} < 0,

which occurs when

\beta_i c_i^{\text{coop}} > v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_j^{\text{coop}} \sqrt{T_{ij} T_{ji}} s_{ij}.

Proof. The total surplus maximization condition equates the sum of marginal utilities across actors to zero (plus shadow prices of constraints), not individual marginal utilities. Therefore individual marginal utilities at the cooperative optimum can be negative, creating an incentive to free-ride. \square

Remark 6.2 (Gap Between Cooperative Optimum and GNE).
The free-riding problem is best characterized as a gap c_i^{\text{GNE}} < c_i^{\text{coop}} under certain conditions. A full characterization is left for future work.

6.2 Contribution-Adjusted Allocation

Definition 6.3 (Contribution-Adjusted Allocation).
Define the allocation:

A_i = \varphi_i(V; c) + \theta (c_i - \bar{c})

where:

· \varphi_i(V; c) is the Shapley value at equilibrium cooperation profile c;
· \bar{c} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_j c_j is average cooperation intensity;
· \theta > 0 is a contribution sensitivity parameter.

Proposition 6.4 (Anti-Free-Riding Effect).
The contribution adjustment shifts incentives:

\frac{\partial A_i}{\partial c_i} = \frac{\partial \varphi_i}{\partial c_i} + \theta \left( 1 - \frac{1}{n} \right).

For sufficiently large \theta, free-riding becomes unprofitable.

Remark 6.5 (Design Trade-off).
The parameter \theta must balance contribution sensitivity against participation incentives. If \theta is too small, free-riding persists. If \theta is too large, weaker actors may be excluded, undermining the heterogeneity that generates complementarity.

Conjecture 6.6 (Truthful Revelation).
Under a suitable mechanism with contribution-adjusted allocation, truth-telling about capabilities is incentive-compatible if \theta satisfies

\theta \le \theta_{\max},

where \theta_{\max} is determined by the curvature of the value function. Full characterization deferred to future work.

