7. Resilience and Infrastructure Redundancy

7.1 Core Resilience under Node Removal

Definition 7.1 (Node Removal Perturbation).
Let \Gamma = (N, V) be a DMCS. For any k \in N, define the perturbed game \Gamma_{-k} = (N \setminus \{k\}, V_{-k}) where:

V_{-k}(S) = V(S) \quad \forall S \subseteq N \setminus \{k\}.

Definition 7.2 (Core Resilience).
The game \Gamma is core-resilient to removal of actor k if the perturbed game \Gamma_{-k} has a non-empty core.

Theorem 7.3 (Core Resilience under Convexity).
If \Gamma is convex, then \Gamma_{-k} is convex for all k \in N, and the core remains non-empty.

Proof. Convexity is hereditary: restricting a convex game to a sub-coalition preserves convexity (Shapley, 1971). Since \Gamma_{-k} is the restriction of \Gamma to N \setminus \{k\}, convexity is preserved, and the core remains non-empty. \square

Corollary 7.4 (Resilience of Quadratic DMCS).
For the quadratic DMCS with non-negative complementarities, the core is non-empty after any single actor's removal.

Proof. By Proposition 3.6, the game is convex. By Theorem 7.3, convexity is preserved under node removal. \square

7.2 Capability Redundancy and Probabilistic Resilience

Definition 7.5 (Critical Capability Set).
Let \mathcal{K}_c \subseteq \{1, \ldots, d\} be the set of survival-critical capability domains.

Definition 7.6 (Capability Redundancy).
For each critical domain k \in \mathcal{K}_c, define redundancy:

r_k = |\{i \in N : K_i^k \ge \kappa_k\}|

where \kappa_k > 0 is a minimal threshold for meaningful contribution.

Proposition 7.7 (Probabilistic Survival of a Single Capability).
Suppose actor failures occur independently with probability p_f. The probability that capability k survives (i.e., at least one actor with K_i^k \ge \kappa_k remains) is:

\Pr(\text{capability } k \text{ survives}) = 1 - p_f^{r_k}.

Proof. Capability k is lost only if all r_k actors with sufficient capability fail simultaneously. Under independence, this occurs with probability p_f^{r_k}. \square

Corollary 7.8 (System Survival under Independence).
If survival events for different capabilities are independent, the probability that all critical capabilities survive is:

\Pr(\text{system survives}) = \prod_{k \in \mathcal{K}_c} (1 - p_f^{r_k}).

Remark 7.9 (Overlapping Capabilities).
In practice, actors may provide multiple critical capabilities, so failures are not independent across capabilities. A more realistic model would use the joint distribution of capability sets. The independence assumption is a simplification for tractability.

Remark 7.10 (Efficiency–Resilience Trade-off).
Let W(r) be the total coalition value with redundancy vector r = (r_1, \ldots, r_d), and C(r) = \sum_k \gamma_k r_k the cost of redundancy. The optimal redundancy solves:

\max_{r} \left[ W(r) - C(r) - \lambda \cdot \Pr(\text{system fails} \mid r) \right]

where \lambda > 0 is a risk aversion parameter. Perfect efficiency (r_k = 1 for all k) is generally not optimal when actors are risk-averse. Some redundancy is always optimal:

\boxed{\text{Efficiency} \neq \text{Resilience}}

