8. Delay-Constrained Coordination

8.1 The Communication Delay Constraint

Definition 8.1 (Decision Horizon).
Each actor has a characteristic decision horizon h_i > 0, representing the timescale over which decisions must be made. If \tau_{ij} > h_i, then real-time coordination between i and j is impossible.

Definition 8.2 (Implementable Strategy).
A cooperation strategy is implementable if all required coordination occurs within the decision horizon:

\tau_{ij} \le \min(h_i, h_j) \quad \text{for all coordination pairs } (i, j).

Remark 8.3 (Delay Parameters).
For Earth–Moon, \tau \approx 1.3 seconds. For Earth–Mars, \tau \in [180, 1320] seconds depending on orbital positions.

8.2 The Two-Layer Necessity Theorem

Theorem 8.4 (Necessity of Locally Autonomous Layer).
Consider a DMCS with actors on Earth, the Moon, and Mars. Suppose:

· Survival-critical decisions have horizon h_{\text{survival}} \ll \tau_{\text{Earth-Mars}};
· Operational decisions have horizon h_{\text{operational}} \gg \tau_{\text{Earth-Mars}}.

Then any implementable governance architecture must contain a locally autonomous decision layer for latency-critical actions, while non-critical coordination may remain globally coupled.

Proof. For survival-critical decisions, implementability requires \tau_{ij} \le h_{\text{survival}}. For Mars-local coordination, \tau_{\text{Mars-Mars}} \ll h_{\text{survival}}, satisfying the constraint. However, for Earth–Mars coordination, \tau_{\text{Earth-Mars}} \gg h_{\text{survival}}, violating the constraint. Therefore, Earth-based actors cannot participate in survival-critical coordination with Mars-based actors. The survival-critical layer must be restricted to Mars-local actors. Conversely, operational decisions with long horizons can accommodate Earth–Mars delay. The separation is necessary: no single architecture includes all actors for all decisions. \square

Remark 8.5 (Relation to Prior Work).
This result formalizes mathematically the “Survival Layer / Operational Layer” distinction proposed independently in Ferguson & Haqq-Misra (2026). Our contribution is the formal necessity proof, derived from delay and decision-horizon assumptions, transforming an institutional intuition into a mathematical theorem.

8.3 Delay and Core Implementability

Definition 8.6 (Effective Core).
The effective core C_{\text{eff}}(\Gamma) is the subset of the core that is implementable under delay constraints:

C_{\text{eff}}(\Gamma) = \{ x \in C(\Gamma) : \text{no blocking coalition } S \text{ can coordinate under delay } \tau \}.

Proposition 8.7 (Delay Expands Effective Core).
As communication delay increases, the effective core expands: some blocking coalitions become infeasible because their members cannot coordinate a deviation.

Proof. A blocking coalition S can only form if its members can coordinate. If the communication graph restricted to S has edges with \tau_{ij} > \min(h_i, h_j), then S cannot coordinate, and the blocking threat is not credible. This removes constraints on the allocation, expanding the effective core. \square

Remark 8.8 (Countervailing Effects).
This counterintuitive result must be qualified: delay may make cooperation easier to sustain in the short run by preventing coordinated defection, but it also reduces monitoring frequency, slowing trust-building and potentially undermining long-run stability. The net effect depends on the balance between these forces, which we defer to future work.

