Appendix A: 
A.1 Proof of Superadditivity (Theorem 3.5)

Theorem 3.5. Consider a DMCS with s_{ij} \ge 0 for all i,j \in N. Then for any fixed cooperation profile c \in \Omega, the game V(\cdot; c) is superadditive.

Detailed Proof.

Let S, T \subseteq N be disjoint coalitions. By Definition 2.3,

V(S \cup T; c) = \sum_{i \in S \cup T} c_i v_i + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S \cup T} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

The first term splits cleanly:

\sum_{i \in S \cup T} c_i v_i = \sum_{i \in S} c_i v_i + \sum_{i \in T} c_i v_i.

For the second term, the set of unordered pairs \{i,j\} \subseteq S \cup T can be partitioned into three disjoint classes:

1. Pairs with both members in S: \{\{i,j\} \subseteq S\};
2. Pairs with both members in T: \{\{i,j\} \subseteq T\};
3. Pairs with one member in S and one in T: \{i \in S, j \in T\}.

Thus,

\sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S \cup T} c_i c_j s_{ij} = \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq S} c_i c_j s_{ij} + \sum_{\{i,j\} \subseteq T} c_i c_j s_{ij} + \sum_{i \in S} \sum_{j \in T} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Combining all terms:

V(S \cup T; c) = V(S; c) + V(T; c) + \sum_{i \in S} \sum_{j \in T} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Since s_{ij} \ge 0 by assumption and c_i, c_j \ge 0 (because \Omega_i \subset \mathbb{R}_+), the cross-term is non-negative. Therefore,

V(S \cup T; c) \ge V(S; c) + V(T; c),

which is superadditivity. \blacksquare

