A.2 Proof of Convexity (Proposition 3.6)

Proposition 3.6. Under the same conditions (s_{ij} \ge 0, c_i \ge 0), the game V(\cdot; c) is convex.

Detailed Proof.

We use the marginal-contribution definition of convexity: for all i \in N and S \subseteq T \subseteq N \setminus \{i\},

V(S \cup \{i\}; c) - V(S; c) \le V(T \cup \{i\}; c) - V(T; c).

Compute the left-hand side:

V(S \cup \{i\}; c) - V(S; c) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in S} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Similarly,

V(T \cup \{i\}; c) - V(T; c) = c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in T} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Subtract the first from the second:

[V(T \cup \{i\}; c) - V(T; c)] - [V(S \cup \{i\}; c) - V(S; c)] = \sum_{j \in T \setminus S} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Since c_i, c_j \ge 0 and s_{ij} \ge 0, this difference is non-negative. Hence,

V(S \cup \{i\}; c) - V(S; c) \le V(T \cup \{i\}; c) - V(T; c),

which is convexity. \blacksquare

