A.3 Derivation of the Shapley Value (Theorem 3.8)

Theorem 3.8. Under Assumption 2.4 (pairwise symmetry), the Shapley value of the quadratic game V(\cdot; c) is

\varphi_i(V; c) = c_i v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Detailed Proof.

The Shapley value for player i is the expected marginal contribution over a random ordering of players, where each ordering is equally likely. For a fixed ordering \pi, let P_i(\pi) denote the set of players preceding i in \pi. The marginal contribution of i under \pi is:

V(P_i(\pi) \cup \{i\}; c) - V(P_i(\pi); c)
= c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in P_i(\pi)} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Taking expectation over all n! orderings:

\varphi_i(V; c) = \frac{1}{n!} \sum_{\pi} \left[ c_i v_i + \sum_{j \in P_i(\pi)} c_i c_j s_{ij} \right].

The first term c_i v_i appears in every ordering, so its contribution is c_i v_i. For the second term, for each fixed j \neq i, the probability that j precedes i in a random ordering is \frac{1}{2} (by symmetry). Therefore,

\mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{j \in P_i(\pi)} c_i c_j s_{ij} \right] = \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij} \cdot \Pr(j \text{ precedes } i) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Combining yields

\varphi_i(V; c) = c_i v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

The use of pairwise symmetry s_{ij} = s_{ji} ensures that the sum is well-defined for the pair, but the derivation does not require it for this formula; symmetry is needed for consistency with the general Shapley value definition when considering arbitrary coalitions. \blacksquare

