Appendix B: Detailed Proofs for Section 4 (Trust Dynamics)

B.1 Derivation of the Bayesian Trust Update (Theorem 4.5)

We restate the likelihoods from Assumption 4.3. Nature first draws a failure indicator F with probability \varepsilon_F independent of type. If no failure, then the signal is C or D with probabilities:

\Pr(C \mid \theta = C, \text{no } F) = 1 - \varepsilon_C, \quad \Pr(D \mid \theta = C, \text{no } F) = \varepsilon_C,

\Pr(C \mid \theta = D, \text{no } F) = q_C, \quad \Pr(D \mid \theta = D, \text{no } F) = 1 - q_C.

Thus, the unconditional likelihoods are:

For o = C:

\Pr(C \mid \theta = C) = (1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - \varepsilon_C),

\Pr(C \mid \theta = D) = (1 - \varepsilon_F) q_C.

For o = D:

\Pr(D \mid \theta = C) = (1 - \varepsilon_F) \varepsilon_C,

\Pr(D \mid \theta = D) = (1 - \varepsilon_F) (1 - q_C).

For o = F:

\Pr(F \mid \theta = C) = \Pr(F \mid \theta = D) = \varepsilon_F.

Given prior T_{ij} = \Pr(\theta = C), Bayes' rule gives posterior T_{ij}^{+} = \Pr(\theta = C \mid o):

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} \Pr(o \mid \theta = C)}{T_{ij} \Pr(o \mid \theta = C) + (1 - T_{ij}) \Pr(o \mid \theta = D)}.

Substituting the above likelihoods yields the three cases in Theorem 4.5.

Case o = C:

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - \varepsilon_C)}{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_F)(1 - \varepsilon_C) + (1 - T_{ij}) (1 - \varepsilon_F) q_C}.

The factor (1 - \varepsilon_F) cancels from numerator and denominator:

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_C)}{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_C) + (1 - T_{ij}) q_C}.

Case o = D:

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_F) \varepsilon_C}{T_{ij} (1 - \varepsilon_F) \varepsilon_C + (1 - T_{ij}) (1 - \varepsilon_F) (1 - q_C)} = \frac{T_{ij} \varepsilon_C}{T_{ij} \varepsilon_C + (1 - T_{ij}) (1 - q_C)}.

Case o = F:

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} \varepsilon_F}{T_{ij} \varepsilon_F + (1 - T_{ij}) \varepsilon_F} = T_{ij}.

This completes the derivation. \blacksquare

---

B.2 Properties of the Trust Update

We note two important properties.

Proposition B.1 (Monotonicity).

· If o = C, then T_{ij}^{+} > T_{ij} if and only if 1 - \varepsilon_C > q_C.
· If o = D, then T_{ij}^{+} < T_{ij} if and only if \varepsilon_C < 1 - q_C.

Proof. For o = C, compare T_{ij}^{+} with T_{ij}:

T_{ij}^{+} > T_{ij} \iff \frac{T_{ij}(1-\varepsilon_C)}{T_{ij}(1-\varepsilon_C)+(1-T_{ij})q_C} > T_{ij}.

Assuming T_{ij} \in (0,1), multiply both sides by positive denominator:

T_{ij}(1-\varepsilon_C) > T_{ij}\left[ T_{ij}(1-\varepsilon_C)+(1-T_{ij})q_C \right].

Divide by T_{ij} > 0:

1-\varepsilon_C > T_{ij}(1-\varepsilon_C)+(1-T_{ij})q_C.

Rearrange:

(1-\varepsilon_C) - q_C > T_{ij}[(1-\varepsilon_C) - q_C].

This holds iff (1-\varepsilon_C) - q_C > 0. The case o = D is analogous. \blacksquare

Proposition B.2 (Absence of Absorbing States).
If \varepsilon_C > 0 and q_C < 1, then no single observation can drive trust to exactly 0 or 1.

Proof. For o = D, the posterior is

T_{ij}^{+} = \frac{T_{ij} \varepsilon_C}{T_{ij} \varepsilon_C + (1 - T_{ij})(1 - q_C)}.

If T_{ij} > 0 and \varepsilon_C > 0, the numerator is positive, so T_{ij}^{+} > 0. If T_{ij} < 1 and 1 - q_C > 0, the denominator exceeds numerator, so T_{ij}^{+} < 1. Similarly for o = C. \blacksquare

