Appendix C: Detailed Proofs for Section 5 (Dynamic Stability)

C.1 Derivation of the Variational Inequality (Proposition 5.5)

For a GNE c^*, each player i solves

\max_{c_i \in \Omega_i(c_{-i}^*)} U_i(c_i, c_{-i}^*; T).

If c^* is interior with respect to local constraints (or with appropriate KKT multipliers for boundary points), the necessary first-order condition is

\frac{\partial U_i}{\partial c_i}(c^*) (c_i - c_i^*) \le 0 \quad \forall c_i \in \Omega_i(c_{-i}^*).

Summing over i and using the definition F_i = \partial U_i / \partial c_i yields

\sum_{i} F_i(c^*) (c_i - c_i^*) \le 0 \quad \forall c \in \Omega,

which is equivalent to

\langle -F(c^*), c - c^* \rangle \ge 0 \quad \forall c \in \Omega.

The shared constraint \Omega_c is incorporated via the projection onto the full feasible set \Omega. This is the standard VI characterization. \blacksquare

C.2 Proof of Strong Monotonicity Condition (Proposition 5.7)

We show that -F is strongly monotone if the stated condition holds. The Jacobian of F has components:

\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial c_i} = -\beta_i, \quad \frac{\partial F_i}{\partial c_j} = \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} \quad (j \neq i).

Let J denote the Jacobian of -F. Then J has diagonal entries \beta_i and off-diagonal entries -\sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}}s_{ij}. The condition \beta_i > \sum_{j \neq i} \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} is strict diagonal dominance of J. By the Gershgorin circle theorem, all eigenvalues of J have positive real parts, and J is positive definite. Thus, (-F)(c) - (-F)(c') = J(c - c') for the linear operator, and strong monotonicity follows from positive definiteness: there exists \mu > 0 such that

\langle (-F)(c) - (-F)(c'), c - c' \rangle \ge \mu \|c - c'\|^2.

This is equivalent to the condition in Theorem 5.6. \blacksquare

C.3 Convergence Proof for Projected Gradient (Theorem 5.9)

We restate the theorem and prove it via contraction.

Theorem 5.9. Consider the iteration

c^{k+1} = \Pi_\Omega [c^k + \alpha F(c^k)].

Suppose -F is strongly monotone with modulus \mu and F is Lipschitz with constant L. If 0 < \alpha < 2\mu/L^2, then \{c^k\} converges to the unique solution of VI(-F, \Omega).

Proof. Define the mapping P(c) = \Pi_\Omega[c + \alpha F(c)]. Since \Pi_\Omega is non-expansive, for any c, d \in \Omega:

\|P(c) - P(d)\|^2 \le \|c - d + \alpha(F(c) - F(d))\|^2.

Expand the right-hand side:

= \|c - d\|^2 + 2\alpha \langle F(c) - F(d), c - d \rangle + \alpha^2 \|F(c) - F(d)\|^2.

By strong monotonicity of -F, we have

\langle F(c) - F(d), c - d \rangle \le -\mu \|c - d\|^2.

By Lipschitz continuity,

\|F(c) - F(d)\|^2 \le L^2 \|c - d\|^2.

Thus,

\|P(c) - P(d)\|^2 \le \|c - d\|^2 - 2\alpha\mu \|c - d\|^2 + \alpha^2 L^2 \|c - d\|^2 = (1 - 2\alpha\mu + \alpha^2 L^2) \|c - d\|^2.

The coefficient is less than 1 if \alpha^2 L^2 - 2\alpha\mu < 0, i.e., \alpha < 2\mu / L^2. Therefore P is a contraction, and by Banach's fixed point theorem, the iteration converges to the unique fixed point c^* which solves the VI. \blacksquare

