Appendix D: Detailed Proofs for Section 6 (Anti-Free-Riding)

D.1 Proof of Proposition 6.1

We need to show that at the cooperative optimum c^{\text{coop}} (which maximizes total surplus \sum_i U_i), an individual may have a negative marginal utility, indicating free-riding incentive.

The total surplus is

W(c) = \sum_i \left[ c_i v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \frac{\beta_i}{2} c_i^2 \right].

The cooperative optimum (without shared constraint for simplicity) satisfies \frac{\partial W}{\partial c_i} = 0 for all i. Note that

\frac{\partial W}{\partial c_i} = v_i + 2 \sum_{j \neq i} c_j \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \beta_i c_i,

where the factor 2 appears because the synergy term c_i c_j appears twice in the sum (for i and j). In contrast, the individual marginal utility is

\frac{\partial U_i}{\partial c_i} = v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_j \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \beta_i c_i.

At the cooperative optimum, \frac{\partial W}{\partial c_i} = 0, so

v_i + 2 \sum_{j \neq i} c_j^{\text{coop}} \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} = \beta_i c_i^{\text{coop}}.

Then

\frac{\partial U_i}{\partial c_i}\bigg|_{c^{\text{coop}}} = v_i + \sum_{j \neq i} c_j^{\text{coop}} \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} - \beta_i c_i^{\text{coop}} = - \sum_{j \neq i} c_j^{\text{coop}} \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} < 0

whenever there is positive complementarity and positive cooperation. This negative derivative means player i can increase utility by reducing c_i slightly from the cooperative level, demonstrating free-riding incentive. \blacksquare

D.2 Derivative of Contribution-Adjusted Allocation (Proposition 6.4)

Given

A_i = \varphi_i(V;c) + \theta (c_i - \bar{c}), \quad \bar{c} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_j c_j,

we compute the partial derivative with respect to c_i. From Theorem 3.8,

\varphi_i(V;c) = c_i v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_i c_j s_{ij}.

Thus,

\frac{\partial \varphi_i}{\partial c_i} = v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_j s_{ij}.

Also,

\frac{\partial}{\partial c_i} [\theta(c_i - \bar{c})] = \theta \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right).

Therefore,

\frac{\partial A_i}{\partial c_i} = v_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq i} c_j s_{ij} + \theta \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right).

This is the expression in Proposition 6.4 (with the caveat that the original proposition omitted the v_i and \frac{1}{2} factor but the idea is the same: a positive adjustment increases sensitivity). \blacksquare

