Appendix F: Detailed Proofs for Section 8 (Delay)

F.1 Proof of Necessity of Locally Autonomous Layer (Theorem 8.4)

Formalize the assumptions:

· A decision is implementable for a set of actors S if for all pairs i,j \in S, the communication delay \tau_{ij} satisfies \tau_{ij} \le \min(h_i, h_j).
· For survival-critical decisions, the decision horizon is h_{\text{survival}} for all actors, and \tau_{\text{Earth-Mars}} \gg h_{\text{survival}}.
· For operational decisions, h_{\text{operational}} \gg \tau_{\text{Earth-Mars}}.

Suppose a governance architecture includes Earth actors in a Mars survival-critical decision. Then the coordinating set includes at least one Earth actor E and one Mars actor M. For this decision to be implementable, we need \tau_{EM} \le h_{\text{survival}}, but by assumption \tau_{EM} \gg h_{\text{survival}}, contradiction. Hence Earth actors cannot be part of the survival-critical layer involving Mars. The only possible actors in that layer are those on Mars (or possibly Moon with small delay, but Moon-Mars delay also matters; the theorem focuses on Earth-Mars). Therefore, any implementable architecture must have a locally autonomous layer for survival-critical actions. Operational decisions, with long horizons, can include Earth because \tau_{EM} \le h_{\text{operational}}. The separation is necessary. \blacksquare

F.2 Proof of Effective Core Expansion (Proposition 8.7)

The core consists of allocations x such that no coalition S can block: \sum_{i \in S} x_i \ge V(S) for all S. In the effective core, we only require this for coalitions that can coordinate under delay constraints. If a coalition S cannot coordinate because some pair in S has delay exceeding their horizons, then S is not a feasible blocking coalition. The constraint \sum_{i \in S} x_i \ge V(S) is removed for that S. Removing constraints can only enlarge the feasible set. Hence C_{\text{eff}} \supseteq C. \blacksquare

