Appendix G: Extended Derivations and Technical Lemmas

G.1 Properties of the Quadratic Value Function

Lemma G.1 (Linearity in c_i). For fixed c_{-i}, the function V(S;c) is affine in c_i. Thus, marginal contribution is linear in c_i.

Proof. From the definition, terms involving c_i are c_i v_i and c_i \sum_{j \in S \setminus \{i\}} c_j s_{ij}, which are linear in c_i. \blacksquare

Lemma G.2 (Trust-Adjusted Game is Convex if \tilde{s}_{ij} \ge 0). The game V(S; c, T) with trust-adjusted complementarities \tilde{s}_{ij} = \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} s_{ij} is convex if \tilde{s}_{ij} \ge 0 for all i,j, by the same proof as Proposition 3.6. \blacksquare

G.2 Monotonicity of Trust-Adjusted Value

If T_{ij} and T_{ji} are non-decreasing over time, then \sqrt{T_{ij}T_{ji}} is non-decreasing, and the trust-adjusted value V(S;c,T) is non-decreasing in time for fixed c. This is intuitive: higher mutual trust increases realized complementarity.

G.3 Notes on Higher-Order Interactions

If we include cubic terms s_{ijk}, superadditivity still holds if all higher-order complementarities are non-negative. Convexity may still hold under additional conditions (e.g., if all marginal contributions are non-decreasing). The Shapley value formula becomes more complex, involving terms like \frac{1}{3} c_i c_j c_k s_{ijk} for each triple, with coefficients depending on the probability that a given subset precedes i. These extensions are left for future work.


